meta {
  name: List Hot Leads
  type: http
  seq: 10
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/leads/hot?limit=5
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

params:query {
  limit: 5
  ~temperature: Hot
  ~scope: own
}

script:post-response {
  // Captured as `hotLeadId`, deliberately NOT `leadId`: `List Leads` captures
  // that one and the Quote Recap / Sold Deal chains depend on it. Clobbering it
  // here would silently retarget those requests at a different lead.
  if (res.getStatus() === 200) {
    const items = res.getBody().items;
    if (items && items.length > 0) {
      bru.setVar("hotLeadId", items[0].id);
    }
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("resolves as its own route, not as GET /leads/:id", function () {
    // `@Get(':id')` matches the literal string `hot` just as happily as an
    // ObjectId. If the controller's declaration order is ever changed, this
    // request starts returning a lead detail (or a 404) instead.
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.have.property("items");
    expect(body).to.not.have.property("contact");
  });

  test("is NOT the paginated envelope", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    for (const key of ["page", "pageSize", "total", "totalPages"]) {
      expect(body).to.not.have.property(key);
    }
  });

  test("rows carry the narrative line", function () {
    const items = res.getBody().items;
    if (items.length > 0) {
      expect(items[0]).to.have.property("lastActivitySummary");
      expect(items[0]).to.have.property("initials");
    }
  });

  test("ordered stalest first", function () {
    const items = res.getBody().items.filter((i) => i.lastActivityAt);
    for (let i = 1; i < items.length; i++) {
      expect(items[i].lastActivityAt >= items[i - 1].lastActivityAt).to.equal(true);
    }
  });

  // The above filters undated rows out, so it passed even when they sorted to
  // the TOP and filled the whole card — which is exactly what happened on
  // migrated data (PAC-80). This is the assertion that would have caught it.
  test("never-touched leads sort after touched ones", function () {
    const items = res.getBody().items;
    const lastDated = items.reduce((acc, i, n) => (i.lastActivityAt ? n : acc), -1);
    const firstUndated = items.findIndex((i) => !i.lastActivityAt);
    if (firstUndated !== -1) {
      expect(lastDated).to.be.below(firstUndated);
    }
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /leads/hot — Priority Contact List (PAC-15)

  The Producer Dashboard's Hot Leads panel.

  - **Module:** `leads` — same gate as the list, because this is a different
    *view* of the caller's leads, not a different resource.
  - **Permission:** `leads:read`
  - **Data scope:** `own` for producers; `scope` is a request that can only narrow.

  ## Why this is not `GET /leads?temperature=Hot`

  1. **The sort is inverted.** `GET /leads` orders `lastActivityAt` *descending*
     — most recently touched first. A priority contact list wants the opposite:
     the lead you last spoke to is the one who least needs a call. It is not the
     same query with a filter on it.
  2. The narrative line needs an `activities` join that the 50-row Leads table
     would pay for on every keystroke of its search box and never render.

  ## Ordering
  Hot leads first, then Warm, and **stalest first within each** — the panel tops
  up with Warm only when the producer has fewer than `limit` Hot leads. That is
  two index-served queries rather than one `$in` plus an in-memory sort, so
  "Hot always outranks Warm" holds by construction.

  Within a temperature, **leads with a `lastActivityAt` come before leads
  without one** (PAC-80). A lead nobody has ever touched has no staleness, only
  a missing field, and Mongo sorts a missing value *before* every real date — so
  on migrated data the 564 never-touched leads filled the card and pushed out
  every lead the producer actually needed to call. Undated rows now follow, in a
  stable `_id` order. Two more index-served queries, for the same reason as
  above: `$ne: null` is a range bound and `: null` an equality bound, both served
  by `{ agencyId, producerId, temperature, lastActivityAt }`.

  Leads in a **terminal status** (Sold, Converted, Not Qualified, Lost, Closed)
  are excluded however hot they were left — expanded through the raw SmartSuite
  choice codes, so a migrated `Lost` lead stored as `jp76g` is caught too.

  ## Query params
  | param | type | default | rules |
  |-------|------|---------|-------|
  | limit | int | 5 | 1–25 |
  | temperature | string[] | `Hot,Warm` | `Hot` \| `Warm` \| `Cold` \| `Unknown`; repeated or comma-separated; **order is priority order** |
  | scope | enum | — | `own` \| `agency`, clamped to the caller's `DataScope` |

  ## Response `200`
  Deliberately **not** the `{page, pageSize, total, totalPages, items}` envelope
  every other list uses — this is a fixed-size card with no page controls, and
  shipping page arithmetic would advertise pagination that does not exist.

  ```json
  {
    "items": [
      {
        "id": "6a71...b87",
        "name": "David Gonzalez",
        "initials": "DG",
        "temperature": "Hot",
        "leadSource": "Mail Referral",
        "status": "Contacted",
        "phone": "(312) 555-8136",
        "email": "david.gonzalez@example.com",
        "lastActivitySummary": "Spouse handles renewals — call back Tuesday AM",
        "lastActivityType": "note",
        "lastActivityAt": "2026-08-06T09:56:53.463Z"
      }
    ]
  }
  ```

  | field | notes |
  |-------|-------|
  | lastActivitySummary | the most recent `activities` row — a mix of system events (`lead_created`, `quoted`, `sold`) and producer-written notes and call logs. `null` when the lead has no activity; the UI falls back to `status` rather than inventing copy |
  | lastActivityAt | the **lead's** `lastActivityAt`, i.e. the field this list is sorted by — not the activity row's timestamp. Showing one while sorting by the other made a correctly-ordered panel look broken |
  | leadSource | normalized label, never a raw choice code |

  ## Errors
  - `400` — `limit` outside 1–25, or an unknown `temperature`.
  - `401` / `403` — as for `GET /leads`.
}
